      "summary": "This document is a synthetic Adverse Event Report Form (Form SYN-AE-01) generated for testing purposes. The reported patient is a 67-year-old male weighing 81 kg with no significant prior liver disease history. The reporter is Dr Liang Wei, a consultant hepatologist physician located in Singapore. The suspect product involved is Hepatrol, administered orally at a dose of 500 mg twice daily starting on 12 January 2026. On 02 February 2026, the patient experienced jaundice, dark urine, fatigue, and deranged liver function tests. The event outcome is documented as recovering. The report classifies the event seriousness as serious due to hospitalisation. Notable findings in the laboratory test table include elevated alanine aminotransferase at 512 U/L and elevated aspartate aminotransferase at 388 U/L. Total bilirubin is also marked high at 78 umol/L, while alkaline phosphatase is high at 142 U/L. Albumin is recorded as normal at 38 g/L. The document lacks information on concomitant medications taken by the patient. Details regarding dechallenge, rechallenge, or drug stop dates are not stated. Specific dates for when the laboratory tests were performed are omitted from the document. Patient identifiers such as initials or record numbers are also not stated.",
